Template: homelab-dns/db_host
Type: string
Default: localhost
Description: PostgreSQL host for the powerdns database:

Template: homelab-dns/db_port
Type: string
Default: 5432
Description: PostgreSQL port:

Template: homelab-dns/db_name
Type: string
Default: powerdns
Description: PostgreSQL database name:

Template: homelab-dns/db_user
Type: string
Default: pdnsuser
Description: PostgreSQL role to connect as:

Template: homelab-dns/db_password
Type: password
Description: PostgreSQL role password:

Template: homelab-dns/role
Type: select
Choices: master, slave
Default: master
Description: PowerDNS role for this server:
 "master" serves as the primary authoritative source and notifies
 slaves of zone changes. "slave" receives zone transfers from a master
 and refuses to originate them further.

Template: homelab-dns/allow_axfr_ips
Type: string
Default:
Description: (master only) IPs allowed to AXFR zone transfers (space-separated):

Template: homelab-dns/also_notify
Type: string
Default:
Description: (master only) IPs to notify on zone changes (space-separated):

Template: homelab-dns/slave_cycle_interval
Type: string
Default: 60
Description: (slave only) Seconds between checking for zone updates:

Template: homelab-dns/disable_axfr
Type: boolean
Default: true
Description: (slave only) Refuse to re-serve zone transfers to further downstream slaves?

Template: homelab-dns/local_address
Type: string
Default:
Description: Explicit IP(s) PowerDNS should bind to (comma-separated), or blank for its own wildcard default:
 Leave blank to preserve PowerDNS's own default listen behavior
 (today's default on every existing install). Set this on a host
 that's internet-reachable (directly, or via a NAT/DNAT forward) so
 the bind is explicit rather than a wildcard -- e.g. "10.50.2.162,
 127.0.0.1".

Template: homelab-dns/enable_api
Type: boolean
Default: false
Description: Enable PowerDNS's own built-in HTTP API on this host?
 Only turn this on when homelab-domain-admin is NOT co-located on this
 same host (the co-located case already gets its own API drop-in from
 homelab-domain-admin itself -- enabling both would double-define the
 same pdns.d directives). Use this when PowerDNS runs on a dedicated
 host and a remote homelab-domain-admin needs to reach its API instead
 (homelab-domain-admin's own pdns_api_host/pdns_api_port/pdns_api_key
 debconf answers point at whatever you set here).

Template: homelab-dns/api_allow_from
Type: string
Default: 127.0.0.1
Description: (if API enabled) IPs allowed to reach the PowerDNS API (space-separated):
 The remote host(s) running homelab-domain-admin. The API bind address
 itself reuses local_address above (falling back to 127.0.0.1 if that
 was left blank) -- there's no separate question for it, to avoid two
 address fields that have to be kept in sync by hand.

Template: homelab-dns/api_key
Type: password
Default:
Description: (if API enabled) PowerDNS API key:
 Leave blank to auto-generate one (printed once at the end of this
 install) -- copy it into the remote homelab-domain-admin host's
 pdns_api_key debconf answer (dpkg-reconfigure homelab-domain-admin).
 Supply a value here instead if you're matching a key that host
 already has.

Template: homelab-dns/proxy_protocol_from
Type: string
Default:
Description: CIDR(s) trusted to send a PROXY protocol header (comma-separated), or blank to disable:
 Set this to the address of a homelab-dnsdist (or any other PROXY
 protocol-aware) edge in front of this host, so PowerDNS sees the real
 client IP instead of the edge's own -- matters for AXFR ACLs and any
 IP-based logging. Confirmed supported by this project's installed
 PowerDNS (5.0.2's own proxy-protocol-from setting). MUST be a real
 CIDR, e.g. "10.50.2.144/32" -- a bare IP with no mask leaves the
 trusted-sender list effectively empty, and PowerDNS then explicitly
 REFUSES every proxy-protocol-wrapped query rather than accepting or
 ignoring it. Leave blank (default, and every existing install's
 current state) if nothing in front of this host speaks PROXY
 protocol -- a query WITHOUT a header at all is accepted normally
 either way; this setting only changes whether a header, when present,
 is honored (trusted sender) or gets the query refused (untrusted).
